---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import CardGrid from '../../../components/CardGrid.astro';
import DocCard from '../../../components/DocCard.astro';
const sidebar = [
  { title: 'Documentation Home', href: '/docs' },
  { title: 'Operations Overview', href: '/docs/operations' },
  { title: 'Two-Host', href: '/docs/operations/two-host' },
  { title: 'Single-Host', href: '/docs/operations/single-host' },
  { title: 'Hardening', href: '/docs/operations/hardening' },
  { title: 'Runbooks', href: '/docs/operations/runbooks', active: true },
];
const toc = [
  { id: 'restart', text: 'Service Restart', level: 2 },
  { id: 'incident', text: 'Incident Response', level: 2 },
  { id: 'issues', text: 'Common Issues', level: 2 },
  { id: 'evidence', text: 'Evidence to Collect', level: 2 },
  { id: 'escalation', text: 'Escalation', level: 2 },
];
---
<DocsLayout title="Troubleshooting & Runbooks" description="Troubleshooting guides and operational runbooks" section="Operations" sidebar={sidebar} toc={toc}>
  <CardGrid columns={3}>
    <DocCard title="Restart safely" icon="activity" tag="Service" description="Stop, clear stale queues or caches only when appropriate, start, verify health and confirm dashboards." meta="Order matters" />
    <DocCard title="Respond" icon="shieldAlert" tag="Incident" description="Detect, triage against decision traces, contain with rate limits or blocks, recover and adjust rules." meta="Four phases" />
    <DocCard title="Diagnose" icon="search" tag="Common issues" description="Suricata silence, stale dashboards, timeline capture, same-host reputation and decay behaviour." meta="Known fixes" />
  </CardGrid>

  <h2 id="restart">Service Restart Runbook</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Stop the affected service.</li>
    <li>Clear stale state (queues, caches) if applicable.</li>
    <li>Start the service.</li>
    <li>Verify health endpoints and logs.</li>
    <li>Confirm dashboards are operational.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="incident">Incident Response Runbook</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Detect:</strong> IDS alerts, high-risk IPs, reportable events.</li>
    <li><strong>Triage:</strong> Correlate with session and pattern data from pattern_analyzer and router.lua decision traces.</li>
    <li><strong>Contain:</strong> Apply rate limits / block lists via IP whitelist or AbuseIPDB reporting.</li>
    <li><strong>Recover:</strong> Review logs and adjust rules.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="issues">Common Issues</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Suricata not producing alerts:</strong> Check rule reload and eve.json pipeline. suricata_rules.extract_alert() returns nil for non-alert events.</li>
    <li><strong>Kibana dashboards stale:</strong> Re-run build_dashboards.py or export_dashboards.py.</li>
    <li><strong>Timeline capture failures:</strong> Verify Kibana auth and embed configuration.</li>
    <li><strong>IP reputation not gating (same-host testing):</strong> Suricata src_ip may be container-internal. Filter private IPs via is_private_ip().</li>
    <li><strong>Fast.log vs eve.json:</strong> Do not regex-parse fast.log. Use structured eve.json.</li>
    <li><strong>Session not recovering after cookie clear:</strong> Passive fingerprint binding (fp_session in Redis) recovers sessions. Check request_fingerprint() and bind_fingerprint_to_session().</li>
    <li><strong>Decay not aging out:</strong> Check score_decay_half_life_seconds — if 0, decay returns stored peak unchanged. Check permaflag thresholds.</li>
    <li><strong>False positives on legitimate traffic:</strong> Honeypot threshold raised from 50 to 80. Consider raising ip_reputation_cap above 50 only if clients don't share addresses.</li>
    <li><strong>Pools not scaling or stuck building:</strong> The pool manager only grows within the budget (<code>POOL_MAX</code>, <code>POOL_MAX_MEMORY_MB</code>, <code>POOL_MAX_CPUS</code>) and above the free-memory floor (<code>POOL_MIN_FREE_MEM_MB</code>); at the ceiling new sessions share ready pools round-robin rather than failing. Check the manager's logs, the budget and <code>POOL_PARALLEL_BUILDS</code>. See <a href="/docs/engine#pools">Engine</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Diagnostic headers missing during a test:</strong> <code>X-Route-Target</code> / <code>X-Risk-Score</code> are emitted only when <code>INTERNAL_TEST_SECRET</code> is set and the request carries a matching <code>X-Internal-Test-Auth</code> header (fail closed). This is intentional — see <a href="/docs/testing/blind-pentest#gating">the test-header oracle</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Host settings warning in a start report:</strong> Elasticsearch needs <code>vm.max_map_count</code> ≥ 262144 and Redis wants <code>vm.overcommit_memory=1</code>. <code>core/host_settings.py</code> checks both and the services report lists any that are missing; set them with <code>sysctl -w</code> (and persist them in <code>/etc/sysctl.d/</code>).</li>
    <li><strong>Edge logs not reaching the SIEM:</strong> check that <code>vector_outbound</code> is running and healthy and points at the SIEM's Elasticsearch. A refused first healthcheck just after start is expected if the SIEM was started later.</li>
    <li><strong>Kibana dashboards missing:</strong> the import runs only after Kibana leaves preboot. If dashboards are still missing, re-run the import job once Kibana answers, or <code>build_dashboards.py</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Pool builds slow:</strong> check <code>POOL_PARALLEL_BUILDS</code> and host memory headroom. Expect about 27&nbsp;s per pool on the reverse-proxy layer and 15&nbsp;s on the database layer; <code>POOL_FAST_CLONE=1</code> is experimental.</li>
    <li><strong><code>down</code> slow or containers killed:</strong> a service without <code>init: true</code> ignores SIGTERM as PID&nbsp;1 and is SIGKILLed after the grace period. Add <code>init: true</code> to the service.</li>
    <li><strong>Failed loads or blank screenshots during a pool build:</strong> Chromium cancels requests on Docker network changes (<code>ERR_NETWORK_CHANGED</code>). Retry; this is not a routing fault. See <a href="/docs/reference/known-issues#operational">Known Issues</a>.</li>
    <li><strong><code>up</code> fails with "Pool overlaps with other one on this address space":</strong> Docker ran out of non-overlapping address pools because a service sat on the implicit <code>default</code> network, which is auto-allocated and can collide with the pinned ones. Every edge network is pinned (172.20&ndash;24.0.0/24) and the SIEM <code>elastic</code> network is pinned to 172.29.0.0/24; <code>init_setup</code> uses <code>network_mode: none</code> so it creates no network at all. If an old remote still has stale empty networks, remove them (<code>docker network rm</code>) and run Start again. A test guards that no stack's subnets overlap and nothing uses the implicit default network.</li>
    <li><strong><code>"VAR" variable is not set</code> warning from compose:</strong> Compose interpolates <code>$VAR</code> even inside YAML block-scalar comments. Write a literal dollar sign as <code>$$</code>, and avoid <code>$NAME</code> in comments.</li>
    <li><strong><code>pool_manager</code>: "No module named state_export" (only on a rebuilt image):</strong> the Dockerfile copies modules by explicit filename (<code>COPY pool_logic.py state_export.py manager.py ./</code>), so a new sibling module must be listed there. A test checks every module <code>manager.py</code> imports is copied.</li>
    <li><strong>Remote Start shows only logs:</strong> older builds cleared the panel when the log tail resumed. The <code>up</code> output (including any build or start error) is now kept, and the tail does not resume if <code>up</code> failed.</li>
    <li><strong>Uploading a few fixed files to a remote:</strong> use <em>Selective upload…</em> in Connectors &amp; Config instead of the full project upload (see <a href="/docs/getting-started/dashboard#remote">Remote Targets</a>).</li>
    <li><strong>Pool &amp; Capacity or Pipeline dashboards empty:</strong> they fill only after the edge stack is redeployed with the pool-state volume and the metrics sources (recreate <code>pool_manager</code> and <code>vector_outbound</code>, restart the SIEM <code>vector_inbound</code>). Detection Coverage stays empty until the dashboard app has pushed the pattern snapshot once (SIEM Analytics &rarr; Send Patterns to SIEM).</li>
    <li><strong>Switching the honeypot layer:</strong> The reverse-proxy and database layers use different compose files (<code>docker-compose.yml</code> vs <code>docker-compose.db-proxy.yml</code>); the active layer is persisted in dashboard state. Bring the current stack down before switching. See <a href="/docs/engine/layers">Honeypot Layers</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="evidence">Evidence to Collect</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Decision trace</strong> — the stage that fired and the accumulated score for the session.</li>
    <li><strong>Suricata alert</strong> — the SID, signature, severity and source address, with the private-IP caveat in mind.</li>
    <li><strong>Session state</strong> — whether the session was bound, which pool, and when the binding was written.</li>
    <li><strong>Pool state</strong> — container health, the assigned database host and whether a build was in progress.</li>
    <li><strong>Log window</strong> — the matching Nginx security events and the SIEM documents they became.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Commands that gather most of it, run from <code>ids/</code>:</p>
  <pre><code># decision trace and security events for one client
docker compose logs --since 1h reverse_proxy | grep SECURITY_EVENT | grep '"ip":"203.0.113.7"'
docker compose exec reverse_proxy grep 203.0.113.7 /var/log/nginx/security.log | tail -20

# session and pool state
REDIS_PW="$(grep ^REDIS_PASSWORD= .env | cut -d= -f2-)"
docker compose exec -T session_store redis-cli -a "$REDIS_PW" --no-auth-warning GET honeypot_pool_session:&lt;SID&gt;
docker compose exec -T session_store redis-cli -a "$REDIS_PW" --no-auth-warning SMEMBERS honeypot_pool:ready
docker compose ps

# full research export (sessions, honeytokens, logs) as NDJSON/CSV
./scripts/collect_data.sh --compress</code></pre>

  <h2 id="escalation">Escalation</h2>
  <p>Escalate to repository maintainers with logs, dashboard state, and alert context.</p>
</DocsLayout>
